C = [
("h1", "10. Sown and Raised"),
("p", "Claim: the second half of 1 Corinthians 15 turns from the fact of the resurrection to the nature of the body, and it does so in answer to a question. The answer is given by a seed, by the differences among bodies, and by four pairs of contrasts. The pairs lead to the sentence on which the second pairing of Adam and Christ rests: there is a natural body, and there is a spiritual body."),
("sub", "10.1 The question (15:35)"),
("p", "“But some man will say, How are the dead raised up? and with what body do they come?” (1 Corinthians 15:35). There are two questions in the verse. The first is about the means: how are the dead raised? The second is about the form: with what body do they come? The first thirty-four verses of the chapter have established the fact of the resurrection. The question now put is of a different kind. It asks what the risen will be like."),
("p", "The answer begins with a rebuke. “Thou fool, that which thou sowest is not quickened, except it die” (1 Corinthians 15:36). The rebuke is short, and it is followed at once by an illustration. The illustration is a grain of wheat."),
("sub", "10.2 The seed (15:36–38)"),
("p", "“And that which thou sowest, thou sowest not that body that shall be, but bare grain, it may chance of wheat, or of some other grain: But God giveth it a body as it hath pleased him, and to every seed his own body” (1 Corinthians 15:37–38). Three points are made. What is sown is not the body that shall be. The seed has to die before it is quickened. And the body that comes is given by God, as it pleases him, and each seed has a body of its own."),
("p", "The illustration answers the question of how and with what body in a way that leaves no room for the objection. The objector wants to know how a dead body can come to life. Paul says that every farmer sows a bare grain and sees a plant. He does not ask the objector to understand the process. He asks him to observe that a thing that is sown dead and bare is given a body that is neither the same as the seed nor unrelated to it. The same grain, and a different body."),
("sub", "10.3 Kinds of flesh and kinds of glory (15:39–41)"),
("p", "Paul then adds that bodies differ among themselves. “All flesh is not the same flesh: but there is one kind of flesh of men, another flesh of beasts, another of fishes, and another of birds” (1 Corinthians 15:39). Then he moves from the flesh to the heavens. “There are also celestial bodies, and bodies terrestrial: but the glory of the celestial is one, and the glory of the terrestrial is another” (1 Corinthians 15:40). “There is one glory of the sun, and another glory of the moon, and another glory of the stars: for one star differeth from another star in glory” (1 Corinthians 15:41)."),
("p", "The point of these three verses is that God makes bodies of more than one kind, and that the bodies differ in glory. A reader who thinks of a body as one kind of thing has not looked at what God has made. The objection that the dead cannot be raised in a body because bodies are all of one sort is answered by the world. There are bodies celestial and bodies terrestrial, and God has made both."),
("sub", "10.4 The four pairs (15:42–44)"),
("p", "“So also is the resurrection of the dead. It is sown in corruption; it is raised in incorruption” (1 Corinthians 15:42). The sentence sets the fact of the resurrection beside the illustration of the seed, and then it gives the pairs, four in all. “It is sown in dishonour; it is raised in glory: it is sown in weakness; it is raised in power” (1 Corinthians 15:43). “It is sown a natural body; it is raised a spiritual body” (1 Corinthians 15:44)."),
("ctbl", "The four pairs of 1 Corinthians 15:42–44.", [
    ("Verse", "Sown", "Raised"),
    ("15:42", "in corruption", "in incorruption"),
    ("15:43", "in dishonour", "in glory"),
    ("15:43", "in weakness", "in power"),
    ("15:44", "a natural body", "a spiritual body"),
  ], [0.18, 0.41, 0.41]),
("p", "The first three pairs are qualities. Corruption gives way to incorruption, dishonour to glory, weakness to power. The fourth pair is the one that names the body. The sown body is “natural.” The raised body is “spiritual.” The King James Version uses the adjective “natural” where a more literal rendering would say //soulish//, that is, belonging to the soul as a living creature, and “spiritual” where the rendering would say “of the spirit.” These are the adjectives Paul will use again in verses 45 to 49, and the second pairing of Adam and Christ will be built on them."),
("sub", "10.5 What “spiritual body” does not say"),
("p", "The phrase “spiritual body” is sometimes read as meaning a body made of spirit, or a body that is not a body. That reading is not required by the words. The paired word “natural” does not mean a body made of soul. It means a body that is governed by the natural life, the life of a creature of earth. By the same pattern, “spiritual” means a body governed by the life of the spirit. The adjectives name what rules the body, and not what the body is made of. The same writer who says, “It is sown a natural body; it is raised a spiritual body,” says in the very next line, “There is a natural body, and there is a spiritual body” (1 Corinthians 15:44). Both are bodies."),
("p", "The question the volume asks of the verse is how it relates to the first pairing. In Romans 5 there was no body at all. The passage spoke of an offence, a judgment, a gift, a reign. Here the passage speaks of a seed, a body, a glory. If the two passages had the same subject, the vocabulary would have been alike. They have different words because they answer different questions."),
("sub", "10.6 The shape of the answer"),
("p", "By the end of verse 44, Paul has given the shape of the answer to the question of verse 35. How are the dead raised? As a seed that dies is quickened. With what body do they come? With a body that is not the body sown but is given by God, and that differs from it as incorruption differs from corruption, glory from dishonour, power from weakness, spiritual from natural. The next five verses will say why Paul can put the matter so, and they will do it by returning to Adam."),
]
